## Mathematical Conventions

All variables are real. The square root is the principal, nonnegative square
root. Thus $\sqrt{u^2}=|u|$, not automatically $u$.
For $u,v\geq0$, $\sqrt{uv}=\sqrt u\sqrt v$; for $u\geq0,v>0$,
$\sqrt{u/v}=\sqrt u/\sqrt v$. Also $r^2-s^2=(r-s)(r+s)$.
Decimal constants are exact rationals. Each requested domain below retains
the original strict or non-strict inequality; a larger natural domain does
not replace the conditions in the exercise.

## 963

All six radicands are positive constants, so there are no variable-domain
restrictions.

### 963.a

Since $196=14^2$, $0.81=(9/10)^2$, and $0.36=(3/5)^2$,
$$
\sqrt{196\cdot0.81\cdot0.36}
=14\cdot\frac9{10}\cdot\frac35
=\frac{189}{25}=7.56.
$$

### 963.b

The adjacent whole number and fraction denote mixed numbers:
$1\frac9{16}=25/16$ and $5\frac49=49/9$. Therefore
$$
\sqrt{\frac{25}{16}\cdot\frac{49}{9}\cdot\frac1{100}}
=\frac54\cdot\frac73\cdot\frac1{10}
=\frac7{24}.
$$

### 963.c

Factor the common $49$ before taking a square root:
$$
\sqrt{0.87\cdot49+0.82\cdot49}
=\sqrt{49(0.87+0.82)}
=\sqrt{49\cdot1.69}
=7\cdot\frac{13}{10}
=\frac{91}{10}=9.1.
$$

### 963.d

$$
\sqrt{1.44\cdot1.21-1.44\cdot0.4}
=\sqrt{1.44(1.21-0.4)}
=\sqrt{1.44\cdot0.81}
=\frac65\cdot\frac9{10}
=\frac{27}{25}=1.08.
$$

### 963.e

$$
\begin{aligned}
146.5^2-109.5^2+27\cdot256
&=(146.5-109.5)(146.5+109.5)+27\cdot256\\
&=37\cdot256+27\cdot256\\
&=64\cdot256=(8\cdot16)^2.
\end{aligned}
$$
The requested principal square root is $128$.

### 963.f

$$
\begin{aligned}
117.5^2-26.5^2-1440
&=(117.5-26.5)(117.5+26.5)-1440\\
&=91\cdot144-10\cdot144\\
&=81\cdot144=(9\cdot12)^2.
\end{aligned}
$$
The requested principal square root is $108$.

## 964

All denominators below are positive and nonzero. All radicands are positive.

### 964.a

$$
\sqrt{\frac{165^2-124^2}{164}}
=\sqrt{\frac{(165-124)(165+124)}{164}}
=\sqrt{\frac{41\cdot289}{4\cdot41}}
=\sqrt{\frac{17^2}{2^2}}
=\frac{17}{2}.
$$

### 964.b

Here $176^2-112^2=64\cdot288=18432>0$ and $98=2\cdot49$:
$$
\sqrt{\frac{98}{176^2-112^2}}
=\sqrt{\frac{98}{64\cdot288}}
=\sqrt{\frac{49}{9216}}
=\sqrt{\frac{7^2}{96^2}}
=\frac7{96}.
$$

### 964.c

$$
\sqrt{\frac{149^2-76^2}{457^2-384^2}}
=\sqrt{\frac{73\cdot225}{73\cdot841}}
=\sqrt{\frac{15^2}{29^2}}
=\frac{15}{29}.
$$

### 964.d

$$
\begin{aligned}
\sqrt{\frac{145.5^2-96.5^2}{193.5^2-31.5^2}}
&=\sqrt{\frac{49\cdot242}{162\cdot225}}\\
&=\sqrt{\frac{49\cdot121}{81\cdot225}}\\
&=\frac{7\cdot11}{9\cdot15}
=\frac{77}{135}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The original denominator is $162\cdot225=36450>0$.

## 965

Every original radicand is nonnegative for all real values of its variables.
The requested domains nevertheless retain the stated strict signs.

### 965.a

Requested domain: $a>0,\ b>0$.
$$
\sqrt{64a^{10}b^6}=\sqrt{(8a^5b^3)^2}
=|8a^5b^3|=8a^5b^3,
$$
because $a^5b^3>0$.

### 965.b

Requested domain: $x>0,\ y>0$.
$$
\sqrt{36x^8y^{36}}=\sqrt{(6x^4y^{18})^2}
=|6x^4y^{18}|=6x^4y^{18}.
$$
Both displayed powers are even and strictly positive on this domain.

### 965.c

Requested domain: $a<0,\ x>0$.
$$
\sqrt{25a^{16}x^4}=\sqrt{(5a^8x^2)^2}
=|5a^8x^2|=5a^8x^2.
$$
Although $a$ is negative, $a^8$ is positive.

### 965.d

Requested domain: $c>0,\ x<0$.
$$
\sqrt{81c^{16}x^{10}}
=|9c^8x^5|
 =9c^8|x^5|
 =-9c^8x^5.
$$
The fifth power $x^5$ is negative, so the leading minus sign is essential.
The result is positive.

### 965.e

Requested domain: $a>0,\ b<0$.
$$
\sqrt{0.01a^{40}b^{12}}
=\sqrt{\left(\frac{a^{20}b^6}{10}\right)^2}
=\left|\frac{a^{20}b^6}{10}\right|
=\frac{a^{20}b^6}{10}.
$$
Both powers are even, so the negative sign of $b$ introduces no minus sign.

### 965.f

Requested domain: $x<0,\ b<0$.
$$
\frac25\sqrt{6.25x^{14}b^{14}}
=\frac25\cdot\frac52|x^7b^7|
=|x^7b^7|=x^7b^7.
$$
Here $6.25=(5/2)^2$, and the product of the two negative seventh powers
is positive.

## 966

### 966.a

Natural domain: $x\ne0$. Requested domain: $x<0$, with zero excluded.
$$
\sqrt{\frac1{9x^6}}
=\frac1{3|x^3|}
=-\frac1{3x^3}.
$$
Since $x^3<0$, $|x^3|=-x^3$ and the result is positive.

### 966.b

Natural domain: $a\in\mathbb R,\ b\ne0$.
Requested domain: $a>0,\ b<0$.
$$
\sqrt{\frac{a^2}{b^4}}
=\frac{|a|}{|b^2|}
=\frac{a}{b^2}.
$$
The denominator $b^2$ is positive even when $b<0$.

### 966.c

Natural domain: $a\ne0,\ b\ne0$. Requested domain: $a>0,\ b>0$.
$$
0.2\sqrt{\frac{16}{a^4b^8}}
=\frac15\cdot\frac4{|a^2b^4|}
=\frac4{5a^2b^4}.
$$
The denominator $a^2b^4$ is positive and nonzero.

### 966.d

Natural domain: $x\in\mathbb R,\ y\ne0$.
Requested domain: $x<0,\ y<0$.
$$
4\sqrt{\frac{x^{10}}{36y^6}}
=4\cdot\frac{|x^5|}{6|y^3|}
=\frac23\cdot\frac{-x^5}{-y^3}
=\frac{2x^5}{3y^3}.
$$
Both odd powers are negative, so their quotient and the result are positive.

## 967

Requested domain: $a>0,\ b>0$. The required product is
$$
\sqrt{ab}=\sqrt a\cdot\sqrt b.
$$
Proof: the two factors exist as positive real numbers. Their product
$s=\sqrt a\sqrt b$ satisfies $s>0$ and $s^2=ab$. Uniqueness of the
nonnegative square root therefore gives $s=\sqrt{ab}$.
The original expression has natural domain $ab\geq0$; this does not
authorize replacing the stipulated positive domain by that larger set.

## 968

Requested domain: $a<0,\ b<0$. The required real quotient is
$$
\sqrt{\frac ab}=\frac{\sqrt{-a}}{\sqrt{-b}}.
$$
Both new radicands are positive and the new denominator is nonzero.
Indeed, $a/b=(-a)/(-b)>0$. The quotient
$s=\sqrt{-a}/\sqrt{-b}$ is positive and satisfies
$s^2=(-a)/(-b)=a/b$, proving the identity by uniqueness of the principal
square root. Writing $\sqrt a/\sqrt b$ would not be a real expression.
The original natural domain is $b\ne0,\ a/b\geq0$; the requested domain
remains $a<0,\ b<0$, excluding both zero endpoints.

## 969

All four original expressions have natural domain $\mathbb R$ in their
single variable. The final forms below are expanded polynomials.

### 969.a

Requested domain: $x\geq2$, including $x=2$.
$$
\sqrt{9(x-2)^2}=3|x-2|=3(x-2)=3x-6.
$$
Here $x-2\geq0$. At the included endpoint $x=2$, both sides equal zero.

### 969.b

Requested domain: $y<3$, not $y\leq3$.
$$
\sqrt{4(y-3)^2}=2|y-3|=2(3-y)=6-2y.
$$
Since $y-3<0$, the result is positive. The endpoint $y=3$ is not included
in the task, even though the identity also has a zero value there.

### 969.c

Requested domain: $a>0$.
$$
\sqrt{a^2(a+1)^4}
=|a|(a+1)^2
=a(a^2+2a+1)
=a^3+2a^2+a.
$$
The squared factor is nonnegative and $|a|=a$; the condition excludes $a=0$.

### 969.d

Requested domain: $b<0$.
$$
\sqrt{b^2(b-1)^4}
=|b|(b-1)^2
=-b(b^2-2b+1)
=-b^3+2b^2-b.
$$
Here $|b|=-b$; the result is positive and $b=0$ remains excluded.

## 970

The final forms have polynomial numerators and polynomial denominators.
No forbidden denominator zero is introduced or canceled away.

### 970.a

Natural domain: $x\ne3$. Requested domain: $x>3$.
$$
\sqrt{\frac1{(x-3)^2}}=\frac1{|x-3|}=\frac1{x-3}.
$$
The denominator is strictly positive. The boundary $x=3$ is undefined,
not an admissible endpoint.

### 970.b

Natural domain: $y\ne-7$. Requested domain: $y<-7$.
$$
\sqrt{\frac9{(y+7)^2}}
=\frac3{|y+7|}
=\frac3{-y-7}
=-\frac3{y+7}.
$$
Since $y+7<0$, the result is positive. The boundary $y=-7$ is undefined.

### 970.c

Natural domain: $p\ne4$. Requested domain: $p<0$.
$$
\sqrt{\frac{p^2}{(p-4)^2}}
=\frac{|p|}{|p-4|}
=\frac{-p}{4-p}
=\frac{p}{p-4}.
$$
Both $p$ and $p-4$ are negative. The fraction is positive and its denominator
is nonzero. The stipulated boundary $p=0$ stays excluded, even though the
original expression is defined there; the natural-domain pole is $p=4$.

### 970.d

Natural domain: $q\ne-5$. Requested domain: $q<-5$.
$$
\sqrt{\frac{q^2}{(q+5)^2}}
=\frac{|q|}{|q+5|}
=\frac{-q}{-q-5}
=\frac{q}{q+5}.
$$
Both numerator and denominator of the final fraction are negative, so the
result is positive. The boundary $q=-5$ is undefined and remains excluded.

